  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 321 PM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nA soggy start to the work week as a widespread shield of rain\ncontinues to move eastwards across Minnesota & Wisconsin. Most \nlocations should end up with at least 0.5 of rain by tonight, \nwhile amounts around 1 & locally higher are more likely across \nsouthern Minnesota into western Wisconsin where showers & \nembedded thunderstorms will linger the longest. Severe weather \nis not a concern given the cool & stable boundary layer, but a \nfew thunderstorms could contain brief heavier downpours. \nSoutheast winds will also remain gusty at 30-35 mph before the \nsurface low moves north of the region tonight & rain ends from \nwest to east.\n\nTuesday looks much quieter as the trailing cold front moves\nthrough the area by around daybreak & drier northwest flow \ndevelops behind it. Breezy west/northwest winds pick up during\nthe morning with gusts of 25-30 mph expected before winds speeds\nbegin to diminish during the afternoon as Canadian high pressure\nenters the region. Afternoon highs will end up around 70 \ndegrees, with prime radiational colling conditions expected \novernight under clear skies & calm winds associated with the \nhigh pressure. Lows in the 30s are possible in more rural areas\nof central Minnesota & western Wisconsin while temperatures fall\ninto the 40s elsewhere. Rain chances dont stay away for long \nthough, as the front begins lifting back north Wednesday ahead \nof another disturbance.\n\nSome scattered rain showers are likely Wednesday afternoon, but\nmore widespread chances for rain come Friday into Saturday as a\nstronger wave ejects out of the Plains & pulls deeper moisture \nnorthwards. Ensemble guidance has trended noticeably wetter with\nthis system, with a widespread soaking rain looking \nincreasingly plausible across at least portions of Minnesota & \nWisconsin. The main question will be exactly where the surface \nboundary sets up, as areas near & north of it could see repeated\nrounds of rain while locations that make it into the warm \nsector could see a few stronger thunderstorms Friday afternoon \nor evening. At this point the heavy rain potential looks like \nthe more likely concern, with the severe threat conditional on \nhow far north the warm front can advance & how much instability \ndevelops. Additional disturbances remain possible over the \nweekend as a stronger upper-level system approaches from the \nwest, so the active & occasionally wet pattern will likely \ncontinue through the end of the seven-day period. Temperatures \nremain fairly seasonable through the week with highs mainly in \nthe 60s to lower 70s.",
